-- Seed users, roles, reviews (+ moods/tags/warnings/helpfuls), and reading statuses.
-- Skips when users already exist (protects existing local DBs).
-- Avatar paths use classpath static /images/avatars/... so clones do not need uploads/.

DO $seed$
BEGIN
  IF EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM public.users LIMIT 1) THEN
    RAISE NOTICE 'Skipping V3 user/review seed: users table is not empty';
    RETURN;
  END IF;

  The book''s greatest strength is its seamless fusion of Shakespearean tragedy with the claustrophobic intensity of a friend group. The students quote the Bard in casual conversation, using his lines to justify their flaws and escalate their rivalries. The drama of Julius Caesar bleeds into their real lives, and the result is a slow, inevitable descent into madness and violence. The characters are deeply flawed and often theatrical to the point of frustration, but that is precisely the point - they are performers who have forgotten how to be anything else.
  The prose is lush and evocative, capturing the gothic, autumnal atmosphere of the conservatory and the creeping dread of a guilt that can never be truly shared. The middle section sags slightly as the narrative juggles its timelines, but the final act is a relentless, heartbreaking crescendo. The ending will stay with you long after you turn the last page - it is quiet, ambiguous, and utterly devastating.', true, 0, 'FAST', 5, 'The central mystery (who killed Richard) unfolds with the logic of a Shakespearean tragedy. The revelation that James delivered the fatal blow, and that Oliver helped cover it up, is less of a twist and more of an inevitability. The novel’s true power lies not in the whodunit but in the psychological aftermath. The final chapter, set ten years later, reveals that James has died by suicide, leaving Oliver alone to grapple with their shared secret and the love they never fully articulated. The queer subtext between Oliver and James is one of the novel’s most poignant threads, and its tragic resolution underlines the book’s core theme: that the roles we are assigned - by others and by ourselves - can become our prisons.', '2026-09-20 21:10:50.042258', 3, 'A gorgeously written, atmospheric tragedy about seven Shakespearean actors whose insular world implodes after a murder. It''s a slow-burn meditation on obsession, guilt, and the stories we tell to survive.', 'Readers who love dark academia, slow burns, rich worlds, and quiet, devastating endings. This is for anyone who ever lost themselves in a play, a friendship, or a story so completely that the lines between art and life began to blur.', 'Anyone looking for a quick, light beach read. Also, be wary if you dislike Shakespeare - the novel is saturated with his words, and the characters frequently speak in his quotes. Readers who need fast-paced, plot-driven action may find the pacing uneven.');
  PERFORM pg_catalog.setval('public.users_id_seq', 9, true);
  PERFORM pg_catalog.setval('public.reviews_id_seq', 13, true);
  PERFORM pg_catalog.setval('public.review_helpfuls_id_seq', 2, true);
  PERFORM pg_catalog.setval('public.user_book_status_id_seq', 24, true);
END
$seed$;
